config MIPK_KEYPOINTS_LAYOUT
    string "Default .keypoints file, empty for none"
    default ""
    help
      Empty, and the sample marks every point where it lies in one colour,
      which needs to know nothing about the subject and so suits any keypoint
      model. Name a .keypoints file and it says which points to draw, in what
      colour and size, and which of them to join into a pose.

      The file also declares how many keypoints the model emits, so a
      different model needs a different file rather than a rebuild. The ones
      shipped beside the program are coco.keypoints for the default person
      model and any stock yolo*-pose, hand.keypoints for the MediaPipe hand
      cascade. --keypoints overrides this per run.

config MIPK_KEYPOINTS_MODEL_PATH
    string "Default video model path"
    default "./person_pose.mipk"

config MIPK_KEYPOINTS_HOST
    string "H264 UDP destination"
    default "192.168.137.1"

config MIPK_KEYPOINTS_PORT
    int "H264 UDP port"
    default 9000
    range 1 65535

config MIPK_KEYPOINTS_GOP
    int "H264 keyframe interval in frames"
    default 30
    range 1 600
    help
      One keyframe per second at 30 fps. A receiver cannot start decoding, or
      recover from loss, until the next keyframe arrives.

config MIPK_KEYPOINTS_REPORT_INTERVAL
    int "Result report interval"
    default 30
    range 1 1000000

config MIPK_KEYPOINTS_KEYPOINT_PERCENT
    int "Keypoint visibility needed to draw it (percent)"
    default 50
    range 0 100
    help
      The model scores every keypoint for whether it is visible. A limb is
      drawn only when both of its ends clear this, so a leg hidden behind the
      body is left out rather than drawn to a guessed position. A .keypoints
      file may set its own per point, and then this is only the fallback.

config MIPK_KEYPOINTS_DRAW_BOX
    bool "Draw the box around each subject"
    default n
    help
      The points alone show where the subject is. The box is useful while
      checking a model, because it shows the detection even when the
      keypoints are too uncertain to draw.

